%S A400634 1828,13820,49566,143610,349822,779502,1584952,3065586,5588308,
%T A400634 9849406,16648452,27450970,43847272,68719374,105008654,158049508,
%U A400634 233008610,339266360,485488250,687518540,959310636,1326698396,1811416750,2454291402,3288192408,4375945868,5765958778,7552628500
%N A400634 Number of semi-meanders with n crossings and winding number n-12, for n >= 12.
%C A400634 A semi-meander is a semi-infinite, self-avoiding, directed curve that crosses a line one or more times.
%C A400634 The winding number of a semi-meander is the hypothetical minimum number of self-crossings that would be created if a ray were to start at the source of the semi-infinite directed curve and extend to infinity without crossing the line.
%C A400634 The maximum winding number of a semi-meander with n crossings is n-2. Thus a(n) counts semi-meanders whose winding number is 10 less than the maximum.
%C A400634 Equivalent to a closed curve (a loop) transversely crossing a ray, considered up to continuous deformation fixing the ray, with a winding number equal to the number of crossings that would be created if the fixed end of the ray were extended to form a line.
%C A400634 Analogized to a river (a semi-meander, meander, or loop) crossing a road (a ray, line, or line segment).
%C A400634 The sixth diagonal of A400429, reading diagonals from the maximal-winding edge.
%H A400634 Hunter Hogan, <a href="/A400634/b400634.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 12..10011</a>
%H A400634 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607039">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, arXiv:hep-th/9607039, 1996 (preprint).
%H A400634 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0550-3213(96)00505-6">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, Nuclear Physics B 482 (1996), no. 3, 497-535.
%H A400634 Hunter Hogan, <a href="https://github.com/hunterhogan/mapFolding">mapFolding: Create, alter, and optimize algorithms to count meanders, stamp foldings, and map foldings</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A400634 Joe Sawada and Roy Li, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/2404">Stamp Foldings, Semi-Meanders, and Open Meanders: Fast Generation Algorithms</a>, The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, Volume 19 No. 2 (2012), #P43 (16 pages).
%F A400634 a(n) = T(n,floor(n/2)-5), where T is the triangle A400429.
%F A400634 More generally, if D_j is the j-th diagonal of A400429, then 2*j is its least index.
%F A400634 For this sequence, j = 6 and 2*j = 12.
%F A400634 D_j(2*j)   = a(12) = A005315(j)   = A005316(2*j-1).
%F A400634 D_j(2*j+1) = a(13) = A005315(j+1) = A005316(2*j+1) = D_{j+1}(2*j+2) = A400635(14).
%e A400634 Row n=16 of A400429 is 110954,427580,349822,135092,27434,2856,134,2, corresponding to winding numbers 0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14. Since n-12=4, a(16)=349822, which is the sixth-to-last value.
%Y A400634 Sixth diagonal of A400429. Cf. A005315, A005316, A400630, A400631, A400632, A400633, A400635, A400636, A400637, A400638.
%K A400634 nonn,new
